{"name":"Wintergreen Mint","hex":"#c6e5ca","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wintergreen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any evergreen plant.","One of various not closely related evergreen plants, including:\nPlants in shrub genus Gaultheria, commonly known as wintergreen, especially:\nGaultheria procumbens, native to North America, having solitary white flowers and aromatic leaves (checkerberry or teaberry)\nGaultheria humifusa – alpine wintergreen\nGaultheria ovatifolia – western teaberry or Oregon spicy wintergreen\nChimaphila maculata – striped wintergreen, pipsissewa\nPlants in any of several other genera in the herbaceous family Ericaceae, including:\nGenus Pyrola, native to northern temperate and Arctic regions.\nGenus Orthilia\nGenus Moneses\nGenus Chimaphila (prince's pine, pipsissewa)\nSome species of the herbaceous genus Trientalis, in family Primulaceae (chickweed wintergreen)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wintergreen"},{"term":"mint","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A building or institution where money (originally, only coins) is produced under government licence.","A vast sum of money;  a large amount of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reproduce (coins), usually en masse, under licence.","To invent; to forge; to fabricate; to fashion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mint"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mint (candy)","blocks":[{"text":"A mint or breath mint is a food item often consumed as an after-meal refreshment or before business and social engagements to improve breath odor. Mints are commonly believed to soothe the stomach, given their association with natural byproducts of the plant genus Mentha. Mints sometimes contain derivatives from plants such as peppermint oil or spearmint oil, or wintergreen from the plant genus Gaultheria. However, many of the most popular mints citing these natural sources contain none in their ingredient list or contain only trace amounts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Scotch_mints.JPG/330px-Scotch_mints.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mint_(candy)","wikidata":"Q5254978"},{"title":"Wintergreen","blocks":[{"text":"Wintergreen is a group of aromatic plants. The term wintergreen once commonly referred to plants that remain green throughout the winter. The term evergreen is now more commonly used for this characteristic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/FountainSpringsWintergreen.png/330px-FountainSpringsWintergreen.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wintergreen","wikidata":"Q1785217"},{"title":"Spring green","blocks":[{"text":"Spring green is a color that was traditionally considered to be on the yellow side of green, but in modern computer systems based on the RGB color model is halfway between cyan and green on the color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_green","wikidata":"Q373787"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/El_Triunfo_Pastillas_de_menta_Envoltura_de_papel_01033B.jpg/500px-El_Triunfo_Pastillas_de_menta_Envoltura_de_papel_01033B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/El_Triunfo_Pastillas_de_menta_Envoltura_de_papel_01033B.jpg/1280px-El_Triunfo_Pastillas_de_menta_Envoltura_de_papel_01033B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/El_Triunfo_Pastillas_de_menta_Envoltur